# BYTHERIM RP — tests

Run everything:

    cd rp
    node tests/run-all.js

One-time setup:

    npm install jsdom papaparse

## What each suite covers

**no-duplicate-members.js** — fails if any object literal defines the same
method twice. JavaScript silently keeps the last one, so a stale copy
overrides working code with no error. This has caused three separate
"blank screen" bugs in this project; it needs no dependencies and runs
instantly, so run it after any large edit.

**league-stats.js** — simulates a regular season and checks team averages
(points, rebounds, assists, steals, blocks, turnovers, fouls, shot volume)
against real NCAA Division I ranges, free-throw shooting by position, and
that games played only counts games a player got on the floor. Catches
tuning changes that push the simulation somewhere unrealistic.

**season-cycle.js** — plays three full seasons with offseasons. Catches
problems that only appear in later years: rosters draining or overflowing,
declared players re-enrolling, offseason stages stalling, rating drift.

**postseason.js** — live bracketology during the season, seeded conference
tournaments, the Selection Sunday show and its Skip button, an NCAA
Tournament played exactly from the announced bracket (1 v 16 … 8 v 9,
regions, First Four), the offseason opening on its own, and the Back button.

**draft-cycle.js** — the pre-draft cycle the Draft RP runs against a save:
combine measurements and athletic testing in real ranges (guards quicker,
bigs longer), personalities and grades, the lottery order, team workouts
that vary by franchise style and personality, the withdrawal deadline,
sixty picks on draft night, the final board kept for history, and the
NCAA RP opening the transfer portal once the draft is done.

**draft-and-save.js** — checks the NBA lottery produces real-world odds,
the mock draft is well formed (60 unique picks, better prospects going
earlier), and the save file stays bounded. That last check exists because
the save once stored every player twice — inside `players` and again
inside each team's roster — which pushed a five-season file near 100 MB.

**save-integrity.js** — confirms everything written to IndexedDB survives
structured clone, and that a save/load round trip restores the universe.
A single function attached to a team object once broke every save in the
app: Dexie raises DataCloneError and aborts the transaction, so the only
symptom was "saves stopped working." Run this after any change that adds
properties to teams, players, or league state.

**rp-integration.js** — plays a season from a copy of the real roster and
recruit sheets (`tests/fixtures/`) and checks what ties the three RP pages
together: the roster sheet's Draft column ("2029 R:1 P:5") makes a player
declare, never withdraw, and go at exactly that pick; draft night is an
offseason stage that gives every pick an NBA team and records the lottery;
drafted players keep their pick; next-season rows at a new school become
scheduled transfers labelled with the season they move into; and the
published snapshot (`data/universe.json`) moves from live to declared to
complete and back to live for the next draft, stays small, and carries
every transfer and each recruit's college seasons. Set `UNIVERSE_OUT` to a
folder to keep the snapshot from each stage.

**rp-pages.js** — boots the Draft RP, the recruiting page and the RP hub
against `tests/fixtures/universe.json`: the stage track and status line,
the seeded mock draft (the same for every visitor) with scripted picks in
their slots, past drafts with NBA teams and logos, the Draft Results tab
once the draft is held, the Transfer Portal (sheet moves, simulated moves,
filters, the Upcoming flag), the "In the NCAA RP" profile section, and the
hub's status lines. Also checks the sim loads the NBA teams before the
engine and has the Publish Universe button.

**recruiting-page.js** — covers `/recruiting`: a failed or empty sheet must
produce a visible message rather than a blank page, school logos resolve
through the alias table (Texas Christian → tcu.png) with a generated badge
fallback, and the dashboard renders quickly. Note it reads `../../recruiting`,
so it only works with the repo laid out as-is.

**recruiting-branding.js** — checks the recruiting page carries the same
universal header and footer as the main site (with paths stepped up one
level), that the section masthead leads with the recruiting logo, that the
accent colour comes from that logo rather than the NCAA RP's blue, and that
school marks render uncropped while player avatars stay circular. Like the
suite above, it reads `../../recruiting`, and takes the main-site nav from
`../../assets/site.js`.

**site-pages.js** — covers the main site (home, podcast, draft, NBA, about).
Every page declares UTF-8, loads the shared `assets/site.css` and
`assets/site.js`, mounts the header first thing in `<body>`, and carries no
inline style blocks or placeholder social links. Each page then boots in a
headless DOM with the Google Sheets and Substack feeds stubbed: header and
footer appear once, the theme toggle and phone menu work (Escape closes it),
feeds and power rankings render, and pages say so when the feed is down. It
also checks how `assets/board.js` cleans the big-board sheet (the `3P&`
header, `-` cells, conference tags, slug-style school names) and that the
draft page's tiers, profiles, stats, search, On the Radar and `#slug` deep
links work, along with the season-by-season stats table (games and minutes
included), the optional sheet
columns (Prev Rank, Wingspan, Comparison, Strengths, Weaknesses, Film), the
`pro.png` logo, the RP hub one folder down, the NCAA RP and Draft RP
using the shared header and footer (assets/chrome.css) with the site fonts
and theme, Basketball-Reference stat rows for linked pros, the X, TikTok and Instagram
cards, tier names and the tier guide, and past boards: the year switcher
(which skips tabs that are still a copy of the current board), the Drafted
column, team logos from names or abbreviations, and year-aware links.

**stats-updater.js** — runs `tools/update-stats.mjs` (the daily job that
pulls last season's college stats from Barttorvik into `data/stats.json`)
against a fake sheet and a fake Barttorvik file, with no network. Checks
exact, nickname and misspelled-name matches (only when the school or
birthday backs them up, including common nicknames like Nate/Nathan), the
"Stats Name" override, transfers, past boards (each gets the season leading
into its draft; unfilled copies of the current board are skipped), and that
freshmen, pros and look-alikes at other schools are skipped. It writes to a
temp file, never the real `data/stats.json`.

## Adding a test

`harness.js` boots the sim against the real `ncaa.html` in a headless DOM:

```js
const { boot, playSeason, playOffseason, ok } = require('./harness');
const { Sim, window } = boot({ roster: csvString });   // omit a sheet to simulate it being down
await Sim.init();
await Sim.startNewGame();
await playSeason(Sim);
ok(Sim.state.ncaaDone, 'season finished');
```
